Lots of us have read the spoilers, compiled by a combination of experience and
people looking in the source code -- e.g. there are spoilers about exactly how
much damage different weapons or spells do, which is detail you can't get
within the game.  On the other hand, you don't really need that level of
detail to succeed.

And apparently some of the major spoiler authors haven't ascended yet
themselves, so such book learning isn't everything...

Without spoilers, one would progress through playing a lot and dying a lot.
Learning through dying.  And consulting the Oracle.  Surprise from me is less
about how far you've progressed -- seems reasonable -- than about a vague
sense that you've gone further faster than many others, who just play and die
a lot for years.

Though to be honest, if you'll forgive me, a lot of the fascination comes from
seeing the contrast of what you have figured out, with what you think you
have figured out, but actually haven't.  Your learning vs. your
misconceptions.  Though some of the latter might be color inserted into your
narratives, or role playing.  But I think most of us don't weep over our dead
pets, let alone bury them.  Might say "darn it!"

